WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.940
Task statement 4.1.

2
00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:08.458
Design prompts with explicit criteria to improve precision and reduce false positives.

3
00:00:08.458 --> 00:00:16.265
A CI reviewer that flags 40 'possible issues' per PR gets ignored, including the one real security bug.

4
00:00:17.665 --> 00:00:24.614
When a review bot is too noisy, the tempting fix is to say be conservative or only flag high confidence issues.

5
00:00:24.614 --> 00:00:29.685
That rarely helps, because Claude still does not know what you consider an issue.

6
00:00:29.685 --> 00:00:36.759
Explicit criteria work like a referee's rulebook: they spell out exactly what counts as a foul and what does not.

7
00:00:38.159 --> 00:00:40.103
Good criteria have four parts.

8
00:00:40.103 --> 00:00:43.344
What to report, with an example for each category.

9
00:00:43.344 --> 00:00:46.650
What to skip, such as formatting or generated code.

10
00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:52.483
What each severity level means, illustrated with real cases instead of words like serious.

11
00:00:52.483 --> 00:00:58.447
And what evidence each finding must include, which cuts down on guesses dressed up as facts.

12
00:00:59.847 --> 00:01:02.449
Developers judge a review bot as a whole.

13
00:01:02.449 --> 00:01:11.652
If one category, say possible null issues, is wrong most of the time, people stop reading all the comments, including the accurate security ones.

14
00:01:11.652 --> 00:01:13.620
So track accuracy per category.

15
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:20.348
Switch off the noisy category while you rewrite its criteria, and keep the trustworthy categories running.

16
00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:27.486
In the continuous integration scenario, the review prompt says to look for potential problems.

17
00:01:27.486 --> 00:01:32.309
Most comments are about possible nulls that the type checker already rules out.

18
00:01:32.309 --> 00:01:35.361
Developers have learned to scroll past everything.

19
00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:43.359
The fix is to pause the noisy category, keep the accurate ones, and write precise criteria with examples before turning it back on.

20
00:01:44.759 --> 00:01:50.275
On the exam, any answer that relies on general caution words is usually a distractor.

21
00:01:50.275 --> 00:01:55.598
Look for the option that defines categories, exclusions and severities concretely.

22
00:01:55.598 --> 00:02:04.684
And if one category is ruining trust, the strong answer pauses that category rather than tolerating the noise or turning off the whole tool.

23
00:02:06.084 --> 00:02:08.024
Precision comes from specifics.

24
00:02:08.024 --> 00:02:15.156
Tell Claude exactly what counts and what does not, define severity with examples, and watch accuracy per category.

25
00:02:15.156 --> 00:02:20.975
When a category is noisy, pause it while you fix it, so the rest of the review stays trusted.

